"At the end of October 2026, Taiwan had 23,204,000 registered residents, according to the Ministry of the Interior. That is about 641 people for every square kilometer, on average. But people are not spread evenly. Some places are packed, and some are almost empty.": "e75490e6f88c",
"At the end of October 2026, Taiwan had 23,204,000 registered residents, according to the Ministry of the Interior. That is about 641 people for every square kilometer, on average. But people are not spread evenly. Some places are packed, and some are almost empty. Look at the cities. The six special municipalities, New Taipei, Taipei, Taoyuan, Taichung, Tainan, and Kaohsiung, are home to almost 70 percent of the people. New Taipei has the most people, about 4 million. Taipei City is the most crowded: almost 8,900 people live on each square kilometer. In Taitung County, the number is about 59. On the 3D model, each column shows how many people live on a small square of land. The tall columns stand on the low land. In this model, land below 100 meters covers about 29 percent of the main island, but about 82 percent of the people live there. Land 1,000 meters and higher covers about 31 percent, yet only about 1 person in every 1,000 lives there. Why? Lessons 9 and 10 give the clues. On low, flat land it is easier to build homes, roads, and factories, to grow food, and to get water from rivers. The Taipei and Taichung basins are flat, with rivers and hills around them. In the model, basins cover only about 5 percent of the land, but about 27 percent of the people live in them. The plains and basins in the west line up from north to south, and so do the big cities. The high-speed rail follows them: Taiwan High Speed Rail says its line is 350 kilometers long and passes through 11 counties and cities in the west. East of the mountains, Yilan, Hualien, and Taitung counties together cover about 28 percent of Taiwan's land, but only about 4 percent of the people live there. The numbers on the model are an estimate made for the whole world, so they do not match the official count exactly. For the main island, the model counts about 4 percent more people than the household registration. But the pattern is clear: Taiwan's people live where the land is low, flat, and close to water. Few people live in the mountains, but few is not none.": "bfcf8dc6a5a8",
